1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,656
By the end of 1918, influenza, and the pneumonia

2
00:00:04,656 --> 00:00:08,540
that came with it, had killed about 45,000 American

3
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:09,225
soldiers.

4
00:00:09,675 --> 00:00:15,147
American combat deaths in the entire war were 53,402.

5
00:00:15,847 --> 00:00:19,767
CDC calls it the most severe pandemic in recent history.

6
00:00:19,767 --> 00:00:22,636
An estimated 500 million people infected.

7
00:00:22,636 --> 00:00:25,155
At least 50 million dead, worldwide.

8
00:00:25,605 --> 00:00:29,168
And in the United States, CDC says, it was first

9
00:00:29,168 --> 00:00:31,617
identified in military personnel.

10
00:00:32,067 --> 00:00:35,199
This is how the deadliest flu in modern history went

11
00:00:35,199 --> 00:00:37,728
to war, and what medicine learned from it.

12
00:00:38,328 --> 00:00:39,655
The flu that went to war.

13
00:00:42,255 --> 00:00:44,691
It started with an army being built in a hurry.

14
00:00:45,791 --> 00:00:48,865
When America entered the First World War, more than

15
00:00:48,865 --> 00:00:50,914
4 million recruits were mobilized.

16
00:00:50,914 --> 00:00:53,807
About thirty training camps were thrown up fast.

17
00:00:53,807 --> 00:00:56,700
Some held up to 50,000 men, in crowded barracks.

18
00:00:57,150 --> 00:01:00,579
One of them was Camp Funston, at Fort Riley, Kansas.

19
00:01:00,579 --> 00:01:03,810
At its peak, it processed nearly 56,000 troops on

20
00:01:03,810 --> 00:01:05,129
their way to France.

21
00:01:05,579 --> 00:01:10,257
In early March, 1918, the first influenza cases were

22
00:01:10,257 --> 00:01:11,786
identified there.

23
00:01:11,786 --> 00:01:14,575
CDC counts more than 100 cases.

24
00:01:14,575 --> 00:01:19,073
Within three weeks, a National Guard history says,

25
00:01:19,073 --> 00:01:23,031
1,100 of the camp's 56,222 troops were sick.

26
00:01:23,631 --> 00:01:26,836
In the weeks that followed, thousands of soldiers

27
00:01:26,836 --> 00:01:29,452
there came down with influenza. 38 died.

28
00:01:29,902 --> 00:01:32,705
Where the virus came from is still debated.

29
00:01:32,705 --> 00:01:35,379
CDC says there is no universal consensus.

30
00:01:35,379 --> 00:01:38,769
One theory, from medical historians, points to rural

31
00:01:38,769 --> 00:01:41,638
Haskell County, Kansas, where a local doctor

32
00:01:41,638 --> 00:01:43,985
reported severe flu early that year.

33
00:01:43,985 --> 00:01:47,311
Young men from that county trained at Camp Funston.

34
00:01:47,761 --> 00:01:51,007
CDC says the mass movement of troops helped spread

35
00:01:51,007 --> 00:01:52,630
the flu around the world.

36
00:01:52,630 --> 00:01:55,616
Within weeks, camps in Georgia reported cases.

37
00:01:55,616 --> 00:01:58,928
By the end of April, 24 of the Army's 36 main camps

38
00:01:58,928 --> 00:02:00,421
had reported outbreaks.

39
00:02:00,421 --> 00:02:03,797
And influenza reached Brest, in France, a major port

40
00:02:03,797 --> 00:02:05,095
for American troops.

41
00:02:05,545 --> 00:02:07,490
And the troops kept moving.

42
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:11,163
In March alone, 84,000 American soldiers sailed for

43
00:02:11,163 --> 00:02:11,668
France.

44
00:02:11,668 --> 00:02:15,341
On one Atlantic crossing, the 15th Cavalry reported

45
00:02:15,341 --> 00:02:17,718
36 influenza cases, and 6 deaths.

46
00:02:18,168 --> 00:02:19,994
That first wave was widespread.

47
00:02:19,994 --> 00:02:21,820
But the worst was still coming.

48
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:24,620
The world would call it the Spanish flu.

49
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:25,828
The name is a misnomer.

50
00:02:26,928 --> 00:02:30,181
Nations fighting the war suppressed press coverage

51
00:02:30,181 --> 00:02:32,458
of the outbreak, to protect morale.

52
00:02:32,458 --> 00:02:33,629
Spain was neutral.

53
00:02:33,629 --> 00:02:36,426
Its newspapers reported the illness freely,

54
00:02:36,426 --> 00:02:39,614
including that King Alfonso XIII had fallen sick.

55
00:02:39,614 --> 00:02:42,802
So it looked as if the flu had started, or was at

56
00:02:42,802 --> 00:02:44,103
its worst, in Spain.

57
00:02:44,553 --> 00:02:47,638
Basically, it gets called the Spanish flu because

58
00:02:47,638 --> 00:02:49,653
the Spanish media did their job.

59
00:02:50,453 --> 00:02:53,069
In the fall of 1918, the flu came back.

60
00:02:54,169 --> 00:02:57,010
CDC says this highly fatal second wave was

61
00:02:57,010 --> 00:03:00,392
responsible for most of the American deaths in the

62
00:03:00,392 --> 00:03:01,001
pandemic.

63
00:03:01,451 --> 00:03:05,255
At Camp Devens, Massachusetts, influenza appeared in

64
00:03:05,255 --> 00:03:05,987
September.

65
00:03:05,987 --> 00:03:10,156
By the end of the month, 14,000 soldiers there were sick.

66
00:03:10,156 --> 00:03:10,815
757 died.

67
00:03:11,265 --> 00:03:15,107
At Camp Upton, on Long Island, the commander shut

68
00:03:15,107 --> 00:03:19,577
the camp to civilian visitors, and ended soldiers' leave.

69
00:03:19,577 --> 00:03:23,654
By the time the epidemic there ended, on October 22,

70
00:03:23,654 --> 00:03:27,653
6,131 soldiers had been hospitalized. 404 had died.

71
00:03:28,103 --> 00:03:32,377
In upstate New York, Camp Syracuse had no hospital,

72
00:03:32,377 --> 00:03:34,220
and only 3 ambulances.

73
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:36,482
It counted 2,289 flu cases.

74
00:03:36,482 --> 00:03:37,990
208 soldiers died.

75
00:03:37,990 --> 00:03:42,347
In the city of Syracuse, about 900 residents died by

76
00:03:42,347 --> 00:03:44,358
the end of the epidemic.

77
00:03:44,808 --> 00:03:47,726
With so many doctors and nurses serving in the

78
00:03:47,726 --> 00:03:50,960
military, CDC says, there were shortages of medical

79
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:52,800
personnel across the country.

80
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:55,844
In Missouri, roughly half of the state's doctors

81
00:03:55,844 --> 00:03:57,683
were serving in the military.

82
00:03:57,683 --> 00:04:00,918
In October, the Army stopped taking in new draftees

83
00:04:00,918 --> 00:04:02,504
until the epidemic ended.

84
00:04:02,954 --> 00:04:04,656
But the war still needed men.

85
00:04:04,656 --> 00:04:07,709
The Army's Surgeon General recommended halting troop

86
00:04:07,709 --> 00:04:08,884
shipments to France.

87
00:04:08,884 --> 00:04:10,410
President Wilson declined.

88
00:04:10,410 --> 00:04:12,700
General Pershing wanted reinforcements.

89
00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:13,639
And they sailed.

90
00:04:14,089 --> 00:04:17,000
These Army films, from 1919, show what those

91
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,521
troopships looked like.

92
00:04:18,521 --> 00:04:21,961
Soldiers packed shoulder to shoulder, for a crossing

93
00:04:21,961 --> 00:04:23,086
of 10 to 14 days.

94
00:04:23,536 --> 00:04:26,622
Healthy soldiers often fell sick on the way.

95
00:04:26,622 --> 00:04:30,200
One sailor counted 15 burials at sea, from a single

96
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:31,392
ship, in one day.

97
00:04:32,192 --> 00:04:33,727
In France, the flu met the war.

98
00:04:34,827 --> 00:04:38,964
In 1918, the American Expeditionary Forces

99
00:04:38,964 --> 00:04:43,298
hospitalized 340,000 soldiers for influenza.

100
00:04:43,298 --> 00:04:47,633
227,000 were hospitalized for battle wounds.

101
00:04:48,083 --> 00:04:51,390
In October, during the Meuse-Argonne offensive,

102
00:04:51,390 --> 00:04:53,641
1,451 Americans died of the flu.

103
00:04:53,641 --> 00:04:57,230
The Third Infantry Division evacuated more soldiers

104
00:04:57,230 --> 00:05:01,029
from the front with influenza than with combat wounds.

105
00:05:01,479 --> 00:05:03,169
The other side noticed.

106
00:05:03,169 --> 00:05:06,843
On October 17, the German general Erich Ludendorff

107
00:05:06,843 --> 00:05:11,105
said the Americans were suffering severely from influenza.

108
00:05:11,555 --> 00:05:13,649
Much of the care fell to nurses.

109
00:05:13,649 --> 00:05:16,921
By the Armistice, the Army Nurse Corps had reached

110
00:05:16,921 --> 00:05:19,408
an estimated peak of more than 21,000.

111
00:05:19,858 --> 00:05:23,258
Historians estimate that more than 200 American

112
00:05:23,258 --> 00:05:25,211
nurses died during the war.

113
00:05:25,211 --> 00:05:28,973
Most of those deaths are attributed to influenza, or

114
00:05:28,973 --> 00:05:31,071
complications like pneumonia.

115
00:05:31,521 --> 00:05:34,636
Back home, Red Cross workers made masks for soldiers

116
00:05:34,636 --> 00:05:35,115
in camp.

117
00:05:35,815 --> 00:05:39,033
And doctors noticed something strange about who was dying.

118
00:05:40,133 --> 00:05:43,965
CDC says mortality was high in children under 5, in

119
00:05:43,965 --> 00:05:47,271
adults 20 to 40, and in people 65 and older.

120
00:05:47,271 --> 00:05:51,103
The high death rate among healthy people aged 20 to

121
00:05:51,103 --> 00:05:54,184
40 was a unique feature of this pandemic.

122
00:05:54,634 --> 00:05:57,921
And the camps were full of young men, an age group

123
00:05:57,921 --> 00:06:00,419
usually less affected by seasonal flu.

124
00:06:00,869 --> 00:06:04,484
And in 1918, there was no vaccine against influenza,

125
00:06:04,484 --> 00:06:08,099
and no antibiotics to treat the bacterial infections

126
00:06:08,099 --> 00:06:09,072
that followed.

127
00:06:09,522 --> 00:06:13,591
90 years later, NIH scientists examined lung tissue

128
00:06:13,591 --> 00:06:17,260
from 58 soldiers who died of influenza at U.S.

129
00:06:17,260 --> 00:06:19,813
military bases in 1918 and 1919.

130
00:06:19,813 --> 00:06:23,722
The tissue had been preserved in paraffin blocks.

131
00:06:23,722 --> 00:06:27,790
They also reviewed more than 8,000 autopsy reports.

132
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:29,427
Their conclusion.

133
00:06:29,427 --> 00:06:32,988
Most victims died of bacterial pneumonia, following

134
00:06:32,988 --> 00:06:35,433
infection with the influenza virus.

135
00:06:35,433 --> 00:06:38,924
The virus damaged the lungs, and bacteria did much

136
00:06:38,924 --> 00:06:39,972
of the killing.

137
00:06:40,772 --> 00:06:43,634
The researchers drew a lesson for the future.

138
00:06:43,634 --> 00:06:46,433
Pandemic planning, they said, should include

139
00:06:46,433 --> 00:06:49,551
stockpiles of antibiotics and bacterial vaccines,

140
00:06:49,551 --> 00:06:52,286
alongside flu vaccines and antiviral drugs.

141
00:06:52,986 --> 00:06:56,220
At home, cities had to fight it without a vaccine or

142
00:06:56,220 --> 00:06:56,966
antibiotics.

143
00:06:58,066 --> 00:07:01,583
CDC lists what they had. Isolation. Quarantine.

144
00:07:01,583 --> 00:07:02,556
Good hygiene.

145
00:07:02,556 --> 00:07:03,604
Disinfectants.

146
00:07:03,604 --> 00:07:05,999
And limits on public gatherings.

147
00:07:05,999 --> 00:07:08,019
They were applied unevenly.

148
00:07:08,469 --> 00:07:11,573
In Seattle, police officers wore masks made by the

149
00:07:11,573 --> 00:07:13,063
local Red Cross chapter.

150
00:07:13,513 --> 00:07:14,641
Timing mattered.

151
00:07:14,641 --> 00:07:18,237
Two NIH-funded studies, published in 2007, compared

152
00:07:18,237 --> 00:07:19,647
American cities. St.

153
00:07:19,647 --> 00:07:23,242
Louis brought in a broad set of measures within two

154
00:07:23,242 --> 00:07:25,569
days of its first reported cases.

155
00:07:25,569 --> 00:07:29,094
Philadelphia waited more than two weeks, and let a

156
00:07:29,094 --> 00:07:31,350
city-wide parade go ahead first.

157
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:33,143
Peak death rates in St.

158
00:07:33,143 --> 00:07:35,478
Louis were one-eighth of Philadelphia's.

159
00:07:35,478 --> 00:07:38,455
Across the cities studied, cities that acted within

160
00:07:38,455 --> 00:07:41,315
a few days had peak weekly death rates up to half

161
00:07:41,315 --> 00:07:43,416
lower than cities that waited weeks.

162
00:07:43,866 --> 00:07:47,376
And then came a third wave, in the winter and spring

163
00:07:47,376 --> 00:07:49,671
of 1919, adding to the death toll.

164
00:07:49,671 --> 00:07:53,045
CDC says the pandemic had subsided by that summer.

165
00:07:53,495 --> 00:07:58,577
Across the United States, about 675,000 people died.

166
00:07:59,477 --> 00:08:03,166
A review in the journal Missouri Medicine notes that

167
00:08:03,166 --> 00:08:06,856
the American toll was greater than the combat deaths

168
00:08:06,856 --> 00:08:07,636
of the U.S.

169
00:08:07,636 --> 00:08:11,751
military in both World Wars, Korea, and Vietnam, combined.

170
00:08:12,651 --> 00:08:14,004
The Army did not forget.

171
00:08:15,104 --> 00:08:18,267
In 1941, it set up a board to investigate and

172
00:08:18,267 --> 00:08:21,501
control influenza and other epidemic diseases.

173
00:08:21,501 --> 00:08:24,665
Its Commission on Influenza was led by Thomas

174
00:08:24,665 --> 00:08:27,688
Francis Jr., at the University of Michigan.

175
00:08:27,688 --> 00:08:30,992
A young researcher, Jonas Salk, ran much of the

176
00:08:30,992 --> 00:08:32,117
day-to-day work.

177
00:08:32,567 --> 00:08:35,620
They built an inactivated flu vaccine.

178
00:08:35,620 --> 00:08:39,397
It was studied in military recruits and college

179
00:08:39,397 --> 00:08:43,575
students, including 12,500 Army trainees, in the flu

180
00:08:43,575 --> 00:08:45,021
season of 1943-44.

181
00:08:45,471 --> 00:08:48,709
The Army ordered every soldier vaccinated in the

182
00:08:48,709 --> 00:08:49,586
fall of 1945.

183
00:08:49,586 --> 00:08:52,284
That November, the vaccine was licensed.

184
00:08:52,734 --> 00:08:55,858
And the military kept watching the virus.

185
00:08:55,858 --> 00:08:58,829
Between 1961 and 1993, the Armed Forces

186
00:08:58,829 --> 00:09:01,723
Epidemiological Board made 53 separate

187
00:09:01,723 --> 00:09:05,685
recommendations on what should go into flu vaccines.

188
00:09:06,135 --> 00:09:08,951
Today, flu shots are an annual requirement for

189
00:09:08,951 --> 00:09:09,991
military members.

190
00:09:10,441 --> 00:09:13,749
And military labs, like this Air Force epidemiology

191
00:09:13,749 --> 00:09:16,927
lab, test samples sent in from military treatment

192
00:09:16,927 --> 00:09:18,742
facilities around the world.

193
00:09:19,192 --> 00:09:21,544
This fall, flu activity rose early.

194
00:09:21,544 --> 00:09:24,232
In late October, CDC said the weekly flu

195
00:09:24,232 --> 00:09:27,591
hospitalization rate was the highest for that week

196
00:09:27,591 --> 00:09:29,271
since the 2010-11 season.

197
00:09:29,271 --> 00:09:31,354
Activity was still low overall.

198
00:09:31,804 --> 00:09:34,683
CDC recommends annual flu vaccination.

199
00:09:34,683 --> 00:09:39,001
Flu vaccines are available for people 6 months and older.

200
00:09:39,801 --> 00:09:43,348
In 1918, flu and pneumonia killed almost as many

201
00:09:43,348 --> 00:09:45,713
American soldiers as combat did.

202
00:09:45,713 --> 00:09:49,259
There was no vaccine, and no antibiotics for the

203
00:09:49,259 --> 00:09:51,033
pneumonia that followed.

204
00:09:51,033 --> 00:09:54,801
27 years later, the first of those answers arrived.

205
00:09:54,801 --> 00:09:58,570
A licensed flu vaccine, built with the Army's help.

206
00:09:59,470 --> 00:10:01,077
Next time on KEPT ALIVE.

207
00:10:01,077 --> 00:10:02,082
The tourniquet.

208
00:10:02,082 --> 00:10:03,823
From feared, to lifesaver.

209
00:10:04,623 --> 00:10:05,764
For education only.

210
00:10:05,764 --> 00:10:07,385
This is not medical advice.

211
00:10:07,385 --> 00:10:10,206
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
